- Support variable row heights.
- Add a sticky header and keyboard navigation.
- When is `memo` harmful?

## Problem statement

Show a todo list and an add form. Adding must update the list immediately without refetching, and roll back if the server rejects it.

## Expected solution

```tsx
import { gql } from '@apollo/client';
import { useMutation, useQuery } from '@apollo/client/react'; // Apollo Client 4; in v3 import hooks from '@apollo/client'
import { useState } from 'react';

interface Todo { __typename: 'Todo'; id: string; title: string; done: boolean }

export const TODOS = gql`
  query Todos { todos { id title done } }
`;
const ADD_TODO = gql`
  mutation AddTodo($title: String!) { addTodo(title: $title) { id title done } }
`;

export function Todos() {
  const { data, loading, error } = useQuery<{ todos: Todo[] }>(TODOS);
  const [title, setTitle] = useState('');
  const [addTodo, { error: addError }] = useMutation<{ addTodo: Todo }, { title: string }>(ADD_TODO, {
    optimisticResponse: (vars) => ({
      addTodo: { __typename: 'Todo', id: `temp-${vars.title}`, title: vars.title, done: false },
    }),
    update(cache, { data: result }) {
      const created = result?.addTodo;
      if (!created) return;
      cache.updateQuery<{ todos: Todo[] }>({ query: TODOS }, (prev) =>
        prev ? { todos: [...prev.todos, created] } : prev,
      );
    },
  });

  if (loading) return <p>Loading…</p>;
  if (error) return <p role="alert">Could not load todos.</p>;
  return (
    <>
      <ul>{data?.todos.map((t) => <li key={t.id}>{t.title}</li>)}</ul>
      <form onSubmit={(e) => { e.preventDefault(); void addTodo({ variables: { title } }).catch(() => {}); setTitle(''); }}>
        <input aria-label="New todo" value={title} onChange={(e) => setTitle(e.target.value)} />
        <button type="submit">Add</button>
      </form>
      {addError && <p role="alert">Could not add todo.</p>}
    </>
  );
}
```

## Explanation

Apollo normalises objects by `__typename` and `id`; including both in the mutation selection lets it store the new todo and keep other queries that reference it consistent. The list query holds an array of references, which does not change by itself when a new object is created, so the `update` function writes the new reference into that query. The optimistic response runs `update` immediately on a temporary layer; when the real response arrives the layer is discarded and `update` runs again with the server data, and on failure the layer is rolled back. Note the hooks' import path differs between major versions.

## Time and space complexity

Cache write is O(n) for the array copy; no network round trip for the list.

## Edge cases

- Query not yet in the cache when `update` runs: `prev` is null, so return it unchanged.
- Duplicate entries if a subscription also adds the item; dedupe by id.
- Optimistic ID differs from the server ID, causing a key change; acceptable for simple lists.
- Network error: the optimistic row disappears and the error shows.

